          estrTitle: "Go Gridas — CRM for Construction & Renovation Companies",
          estrMeta: "A self-hosted CRM that tracks every job from start to payout — what work was done, at which property, by which crew, what was spent, what was earned, and what each employee gets paid.",
          demoNote: "Screenshots use demo data — names, addresses and figures are made up.",
          estrValueHead: "Your business",
          estrDailyHead: "Day-to-day work in the system",
          estrValueLead: "Most small companies run on gut feel: it seems like Rīga brings the most work, that one crew performs better, that materials cost about this much. As long as the numbers live in someone's head and on paper, none of it can be checked. These nine reports build themselves from everyday data and answer questions that otherwise get answered by guesswork.",
          estrDailyLead: "None of these reports is extra work. They come from what the crew records anyway: which site, what job, how many square metres, at what rate, who worked on it. The data goes in once, right where the work happens, and adds itself up into the reports.",
          estrValueQ1: "Where is the money actually made?",
          estrValueT1: "Turnover on its own tells you nothing. In Rīga 41 jobs return 29.5%; in Jūrmala 7 jobs return 21.9%. Until those numbers are separated, ad budget and time get spread blindly. Split out, it shows where to push harder, where to raise prices, and what to walk away from.",
          estrValueQ2: "Will there be enough cash next month?",
          estrValueT2: "Profit on paper and money in the account are not the same thing. In February €22,156 came in while €34,881 went out to the crew — nearly €15,000 negative in a single month. A hole like that stays invisible until there is nothing to pay with. Broken down by month, it shows up early enough to move a purchase, ask for an advance, or not take on a job at that exact moment.",
          estrValueQ3: "How seasonal is the business, really?",
          estrValueT3: "69% of yearly revenue happens in five summer months, the quiet season brings just 9%. Without that number it's hard to know how big a reserve to set aside before the season ends.",
          estrValueQ4: "Which month to actually start preparing in?",
          estrValueT4: "The gap between the strongest and weakest month is over 16x — June brings €403,014 a year, February just €24,563. With the month-by-month picture, staffing and materials can be scaled up ahead of time instead of reacting once orders already arrive.",
          estrValueQ5: "How do you keep people through the slow season?",
          estrValueT5: "The system automatically holds back 4–10% of every payout into a reserve, so that in winter there's something to pay staff from and they don't go looking for work elsewhere.",
          estrValueQ6: "Does this actually work, or does it just look good on paper?",
          estrValueT6: "The payout history shows real disbursements across several years — at Christmas, at the end of the autumn season, in the February off-season. It's not a theoretical calculation — the money reaches employees automatically every time.",
          estrValueQ7: "How much does each worker actually produce?",
          estrValueT7: "With one crew you can still keep everyone's output and deductions in your head. With two it gets tight; with three it stops working — and bonuses become a matter of relations rather than numbers. With output and payouts side by side, it shows who can take on more, who needs help, and whether a new hire is needed. That is exactly what lets the company grow past what one person can hold personally.",
          estrValueQ8: "How much material to buy for next season?",
          estrValueT8: "541 entries across 12 material types and 74 sites — nobody keeps that in their head. When consumption is tied to job type and area, the season's purchasing can be calculated instead of guessed: less cash frozen in the warehouse, and fewer stoppages because material ran out mid-job.",
          estrValueQ9: "How much money leaks through unnoticed pricing deviations?",
          estrValueT9: "For the same type of work (say, Estrich), the rate charged across sites ranges from €3.06 to €25.00 per m². With a handful of sites you can hold every price in your head — with 74 a year, you can't. The system automatically finds 65 line items billed below 60% of that work type's average rate — €10,287.64 combined, over 4% of annual profit. Without tracking, these gaps surface only at year-end, once it's too late to fix — with an alert, they can be caught while the job is still running.",
          estrB1: "Overview — central dashboard with date-range filtering, summary totals, and tabbed tables across all data types",
